WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:08.100
Death comes to us all when we game, presenting
us with anger-inducing Game Over screens and

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.960
the feeling of defeat. Sometimes, those deaths
are well-deserved, but others are just plain

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.740
dumb, the result of stupidity or simply bad
luck. Oh, and just make note that some of

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:22.060
these deaths can be pretty gruesome and are
not exactly safe for work. Unless you work

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:23.849
as a butcher or something.

00:00:23.849 --> 00:00:25.270
Death by Cuccos

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:29.239
We'll start off with a classic. If you've
been gaming for a while, then you've probably

00:00:29.239 --> 00:00:33.410
played the Legend of Zelda series. And if
you're familiar with Link's adventures, then

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:38.520
you're probably also familiar with his many
enemies. It turns out, however, that the humble

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:44.000
chicken is a foe that can give even Ganon
a run for his money. Antagonizing a Cucco

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.880
can bring down a fowl doom upon our intrepid
hero. Don't poke the chickens. They will poke

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:55.430
back—with a vengeance, and across platforms.
They do not forgive, and they do not forget…

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.329
until you leave the village, at least.

00:00:57.329 --> 00:00:58.430
Hit and Run

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:03.239
There's a wide arsenal of available weaponry
in Grand Theft Auto V, ranging from small

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:08.150
firearms to the death-bringing Rhino Tank,
and with so many tools at your disposal, you'd

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:11.920
think that the dumbest way to die in this
game would involve some kind of rocket launcher

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.050
mishap or a grenade bouncing back, right?
Nope.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:19.970
Just like real life, it turns out that all
you need to suffer a meaningless death is

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:24.140
to be on the street at the wrong place and
time, because non-player characters have no

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:29.440
problem committing a remorseless hit and run.
This is why we look both ways before crossing

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.670
the street...to kill a guy with an uzi.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.299
Waterslide of death

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:39.600
The 2013 reboot of Tomb Raider introduced
us to a young Lara Croft, who was only just

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.720
becoming the hardy adventurer we know today.
Lara suffers many hardships and fights against

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.310
enemies on Yamatai Island, but no amount of
shimmying and shooting can save her from the

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.890
treacherous terrain. Every now and then, there's
a section of the game that has Croft tumbling

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:58.939
down a steep hill or being washed downstream
in violent rapids. If you aren't careful and

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:04.380
fail to steer her towards safety, Lara will
meet an extraordinarily grim end. Look away

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.080
now, kids.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.540
"Oh no!"

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:12.760
Maybe just stick to tombs next time.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:13.760
Overcooked Grenades

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.669
Anyone who’s played a first-person shooter
within the last few years should be familiar

00:02:17.669 --> 00:02:22.419
with the concept of "cooking" a grenade. This
involves holding an unpinned grenade in your

00:02:22.419 --> 00:02:26.900
hand for a bit before chucking it at your
enemy so that it explodes on impact instead

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.230
of bouncing around on the ground. Sometimes,
though, players forget the whole "throw it

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:35.590
at the enemy" part. Once you've overcooked
a grenade, chances are that you’re the one

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:37.670
who’s going to be well done.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:39.760
(boom)

00:02:40.409 --> 00:02:44.290
Explosives have a way of doing more damage
to your dignity than your body, as we’ll

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.939
see in our next example.

00:02:46.939 --> 00:02:47.829
Rocket Jump Fail

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:53.519
The rocket jump is a skill employed by many
advanced players in Team Fortress 2. The Soldier,

00:02:53.519 --> 00:02:57.930
as dimwitted as he is, can use his rocket
launcher to propel himself forward by firing

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:02.840
it at his feet and jumping at the same time.
It’s a technique that many players master,

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.109
even though it can actually damage or kill
you, either from the blast or jumping so high

00:03:07.109 --> 00:03:12.269
that your legs break upon your return to Earth.
Unfortunate players can also launch themselves

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:16.739
over cliffs, which is humiliating, especially
when the game announces that you've fallen

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:18.760
to a clumsy death.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:19.989
Careless Package

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:24.239
Getting a small killstreak and receiving a
care package in the Call of Duty series is

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:28.749
a pretty good feeling. This incentive gives
players more of a cause to play smart and

00:03:28.749 --> 00:03:33.529
stay alive, but sometimes, players end up
being killed by their own care packages, which

00:03:33.529 --> 00:03:38.309
are known to drop out of the sky hard and
fast. There are few things more embarrassing

00:03:38.309 --> 00:03:42.639
than being killed by your own reward… except
for the instant replay you’re treated to

00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:44.439
moments later.

00:03:44.439 --> 00:03:45.449
Fire Walk

00:03:45.449 --> 00:03:49.900
This one is a no-brainer, but many players
still need to be told that fire will kill

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:53.870
them. In World of Warcraft's dungeons and
raids, bosses usually cover the ground with

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:58.979
fire, acid, or some other kind of goop that
reduces your health. A dungeon run could be

00:03:58.979 --> 00:04:03.579
going exceptionally well but quickly turn
into a huge mess if someone isn't paying attention

00:04:03.579 --> 00:04:07.989
to where they're standing. A good rule of
thumb is that if it looks like it could kill

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:12.870
you, it probably will, so do yourself a favor
and don’t stand in fire. Unless, of course,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.449
you’re going for the suicidal “Stood in
Fire” achievement, in which case, knock

00:04:16.449 --> 00:04:18.639
yourself out. Literally.

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:20.959
Speaking of playing with fire...

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:22.330
Romancing Morinth

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:28.610
One small, stupid, kinda gross choice in Mass
Effect 2 can end your adventure immediately.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:32.580
As Commander Shepard, you have the option
of trying to seduce any of your crewmates,

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:37.220
including the strange and dangerous Morinth,
who’s kind of like an alien succubus. At

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.250
one point, she admits to Shepard that she
absorbs the life force from her lovers and

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:46.490
leaves them black widow-style, empty and dead.
Shepard can pay her a visit and try to engage

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:51.310
in a little intergalactic hanky panky, but
he’ll immediately suffer a soul-sucking

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:55.780
death, which is probably not worth it. Probably.
So just keep it in your pants and save the

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:57.340
galaxy instead.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:58.690
Impatient Sonic

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.730
Patience is a virtue... except when you’re
playing Sonic CD. If you leave your controller

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:08.190
alone and let Sonic stand still for a little
under three minutes, he'll get so sick of

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:12.620
you that he’ll kill himself by jumping out
of the game, followed by some really creepy

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.600
funeral music. See for yourself:

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:22.340
"I'm outa here!"

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:25.040
"Yay!"

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:33.230
As far as video game deaths go, this one feels
like one of the most offbeat and unnecessary.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:38.000
Why couldn’t he just skip rope with his
own head like Earthworm Jim?

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.320
Quick Time Events

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.970
We've finally reached the bane of many a player's
existence, and the worst of all video game

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:49.350
evils: the quick time event. In Resident Evil
5 and 6, as well as countless other modern

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.690
games, you're occasionally presented with
an obstacle that requires you to press specific

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:59.380
buttons at exactly the right time. Failure
to do so results in an immediate death.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:03.920
The later Resident Evil games are so bogged
down by these unnecessary quick time events

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:08.200
that it's impossible to avoid at least one
or two brutal deaths starring your favorite

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:13.190
zombie slayers. You'll be caught up in a cutscene
and be totally unprepared for the sudden event,

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:17.430
leading to a Game Over and the annoyance of
having to watch the same scene over again

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:21.480
until you get to the part where you died.
Why are we pressing ‘A’ to run instead

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:25.780
of, you know, actually running anyhow? You’d
better train up your reflexes, or you'll end

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:28.550
up reliving your failures over and over again.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:32.760
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.890
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know the dumbest ways you’ve ever died

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:37.720
in a video game...

